# Clipur Media Command — environment template
#
# Copy to .env.local for local development:
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret. Never commit a real .env.local, and never
# put a Turso auth token in this file.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database --
#
# Leave both unset for local development: the app falls back to a SQLite file
# at ./data/clipur.db and behaves exactly as it does on a laptop.
#
# For a hosted deployment (Vercel, or anywhere serverless) BOTH are required.
# A serverless host has no writable, persistent filesystem, so the local-file
# fallback silently loses every write there.
#
#   1. turso db create clipur
#   2. turso db show clipur --url        -> TURSO_DATABASE_URL
#   3. turso db tokens create clipur     -> TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN
#
# TURSO_DATABASE_URL=libsql://your-database-name-you.turso.io
# TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=your-token-here


# Overrides the local SQLite file location. Ignored when TURSO_DATABASE_URL is
# set. Useful when the database must live on a mounted volume.
# CLIPUR_DB_PATH=./data/clipur.db


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- content --

# The shared Drive folder the media team works out of, linked in the header.
# Defaults to the Clipur content folder hard-coded in lib/config.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTENT_FOLDER_URL=https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/REPLACE_ME


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- notes --
#
# NODE_ENV is set by the npm scripts (dev / build / start). Do not set it here.
#
# Session cookies are marked Secure when NODE_ENV=production, so a production
# deployment must be served over HTTPS or nobody can stay signed in. Vercel
# provides HTTPS automatically.
